INT. BLACKWATER SOUND - LIVE ROOM - NIGHT

A converted cannery. Brick, baffles, foam. Forty-eight channels on the console in the control room beyond the glass, every fader at unity. The red ON AIR light burns steadily over the door.

Outside, a storm has been building since sunset. Wind leans on the roll-up door. Water beads along the skylight seams.

THE BAND stands at their marks: drummer behind the kit, bassist with headphones half on, two guitarists sharing one tuner, a keyboardist leaning on a Rhodes. Take fourteen of "Low Tide." Slate called. Click track in their cans.

THE BAND
(counting in, together)
One, two, three, four.

They play. Tight. Almost right. The drummer drops a fill, the bassist drifts a hair behind the grid, and the track grinds to a halt on the bridge.

THE BAND
Stop. Stop. Sorry.

Talkback is dead. The glass shows only their reflection and the dark behind it.

The click track keeps ticking in every headphone. Tick. Tick. Tick.

THE BAND
Engineer? Can we get a playback?

Nothing. The monitors hiss at -40 dB noise floor. The patch bay shows a row of green LEDs, then one by one they go amber.

Rain begins to find a way through the roof. A drop lands on the snare. Another on the Rhodes lid.

THE BAND
Somebody check the breaker.

The guitarists unplug and cross to the panel by the loading door. The drummer lifts his sticks and freezes. The bassist freezes too. The click track stops.

All five go still.

Low in the walls, an electrical whine climbs. Pitch rising. Rising.

THE BAND
That's the transformer. Pull the plugs. Pull everything.

They scramble. The keyboardist yanks the Rhodes cable, the bassist rips his headphones off, the drummer dives for the floor tom, and two of them collide at the gear cart, tangle in a snake of XLR cables, and go down together, mics clattering around them.

THE BAND
Get up! Get off the mats, it's the floor, it's wet!

Water has pooled under the loading door and spreads across the hardwood in a thin black sheet toward the power strips.

Silence. Drip. Drip.

Then a hum. Deep. Everything in the room powers up at once, console, amps, monitors, and the speakers SCREAM a wall of feedback that shakes the foam from the ceiling.

THE BAND
Out! Everybody out, now!

They run. The group scatters for the emergency exit, one toward the stage door, two to the control room, one back for the master tape. The master tape wins. He grabs the reel under his arm.

A SPARK jumps from the strip to the water.

The overhead lights BURST one after another, glass raining down, and the whole live room goes white, then black, then white.

THE BAND
Go! Go! Go!

The fire door SLAMS open on the storm. They pour into the rain, drenched, shouting names into the wind, and behind them the cannery windows flash blue and die.

THE BAND
Count heads!

Five. Five. They count again to be sure.
